# PowerShell and Batch Script Standards

## PowerShell Scripts (.ps1)
- Use `Write-Host` with color coding for user feedback
- Include error handling with `try-catch` blocks
- Use `Set-Location` to ensure correct working directory
- Provide clear usage instructions and examples
- Use parameter validation and help documentation

## Batch Scripts (.bat)
- Include `@echo off` at the beginning
- Use descriptive echo statements for user feedback
- Handle error levels with `if %errorlevel%` checks
- Use `cd /d` for directory changes with drive support
- Include pause statements for user interaction when needed

## Common Patterns
- Always change to the project directory: `cd /d "C:\Users\Berries\Documents\EE WIFI"`
- Check for administrator privileges when needed
- Provide fallback options for common errors
- Use consistent error messaging format

## Service Management
- Use `Stop-Service` and `Start-Service` for Windows services
- Include service status checks before operations
- Handle service installation/uninstallation errors
- Use `sc.exe` for advanced service operations

## Firewall Rules
- Use `New-NetFirewallRule` for PowerShell firewall management
- Use `netsh advfirewall` for batch script firewall rules
- Always check if rules already exist before creating
- Include descriptive rule names and comments

## Example Patterns
```powershell
try {
    Write-Host "Starting operation..." -ForegroundColor Green
    Set-Location "C:\Users\Berries\Documents\EE WIFI"
    # Operation code here
} catch {
    Write-Host "Error: $($_.Exception.Message)" -ForegroundColor Red
} finally {
    Write-Host "Operation complete!" -ForegroundColor Green
}
```